<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garhwal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Garhwal is the western of the two divisions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, containing the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedarnath">Kedarnath</a>. It took its name from the many forts (garh) of local chiefs, united by the Panwar dynasty. After Gorkha rule from 1804 to 1815 it was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-garhwal">British Garhwal</a> and the princely state of Tehri Garhwal.</p></div>
